## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates understanding of the Trie (prefix tree) data structure, covering insert, delete, and prefix-query operations with proper edge case handling.

## Key Concepts

- **Trie node structure**: Each node stores 26 child pointers, an end-of-word flag, and a pass-through counter for efficient prefix queries
- **Safe deletion**: Decrement counts along the path and only unmark end-of-word when the word actually exists
- **Prefix query**: Return the count at the prefix's last node in O(length of prefix) time
- **Duplicate handling**: Adding the same word twice increments the counter twice; deleting decrements twice, effectively removing one occurrence

## Code Example

```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.children = new Array(26).fill(null);
    this.isEndOfWord = false;
    this.count = 0;
  }
}

class Trie {
  constructor() {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
  }

  add(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of word) {
      const index = char.charCodeAt(0) - 97;
      if (!node.children[index]) {
        node.children[index] = new TrieNode();
      }
      node = node.children[index];
      node.count++;
    }
    node.isEndOfWord = true;
  }

  delete(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of word) {
      const index = char.charCodeAt(0) - 97;
      if (!node.children[index]) return;
      node = node.children[index];
      node.count--;
    }
    node.isEndOfWord = false;
  }

  countPrefix(prefix) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of prefix) {
      const index = char.charCodeAt(0) - 97;
      if (!node.children[index]) return 0;
      node = node.children[index];
    }
    return node.count;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to blend topological ordering with DP to compute the longest-path in a weighted DAG and count the number of critical paths, including multiple sources and modulo arithmetic.

## Key Concepts
- Longest-path in DAG with node durations and edge setup weights
- Topological sort (Kahn) to process in dependency order
- DP for counting number of longest paths with modulo

## Code Example
```javascript
function countCriticalPaths(N, edges, dur) {
  const MOD = 1000000007;
  const incoming = Array.from({length: N}, () => []);
  const indeg = Array(N).fill(0);

  for (const [u, v, w] of edges) {
    incoming[v].push([u, w]);
    indeg[v]++;
  }

  const EFT = dur.slice();
  const count = Array(N).fill(0);
  const q = [];

  for (let i = 0; i < N; i++) {
    if (indeg[i] === 0) {
      count[i] = 1;
      q.push(i);
    }
  }

  while (q.length) {
    const u = q.shift();
    for (const [v, w] of incoming[u]) {
      const newEFT = EFT[u] + w + dur[v];
      if (newEFT > EFT[v]) {
        EFT[v] = newEFT;
        count[v] = count[u];
      } else if (newEFT === EFT[v]) {
        count[v] = (count[v] + count[u]) % MOD;
      }
      indeg[u]--;
      if (indeg[u] === 0) q.push(u);
    }
  }

  let maxEFT = 0, totalCritical = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < N; i++) {
    if (EFT[i] > maxEFT) {
      maxEFT = EFT[i];
      totalCritical = count[i];
    } else if (EFT[i] === maxEFT) {
      totalCritical = (totalCritical + count[i]) % MOD;
    }
  }
  return { EFT, maxEFT, criticalPaths: totalCritical };
}
```

## Complexity
- Time: O(N + M) for topological sort and DP
- Space: O(N + M) for adjacency and arrays

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests dynamic programming skills on a directed acyclic graph (DAG) with multiple predecessor choices and path counting for optimal solutions. The ability to handle several predecessors, implement proper tie-breaking for path counting, and correctly apply modulo arithmetic demonstrates code clarity and robustness with edge cases.

## Key Concepts
- DP on DAG with 3-step transitions (i → i+1, i+2, i+3)
- Maintaining both the optimal value and the count of optimal paths
- Modulo arithmetic (1e9+7) for path counting
- Edge-case handling for small n and negative profit values

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxProfitAndPaths(profits) {
  const n = profits.length;
  const MOD = 1000000007n;
  if (n === 0) return { best: 0, cnt: 1 };
  if (n === 1) return { best: profits[0], cnt: 1 };

  const best = new Array(n).fill(0);
  const cnt = new Array(n).fill(0);
  best[0] = profits[0];
  cnt[0] = 1;

  for (let i = 1; i < n; i++) {
    let maxVal = -Infinity;
    for (const p of [i-1, i-2, i-3]) {
      if (p >= 0) {
        const cand = best[p] + profits[i];
        if (cand > maxVal) {
          maxVal = cand;
          cnt[i] = cnt[p];
        } else if (cand === maxVal) {
          cnt[i] = (cnt[i] + cnt[p]) % MOD;
        }
      }
    }
    best[i] = maxVal;
  }
  return { best: best[n-1], cnt: cnt[n-1] };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to design algorithms with provable performance guarantees, handle pathological inputs gracefully, and understand trade-offs between different sorting approaches. It's particularly relevant for large-scale systems where worst-case behavior impacts reliability.

## Key Concepts
- **Introsort**: Hybrid algorithm combining quicksort's average-case efficiency with heapsort's worst-case guarantees
- **3-way partitioning**: Dutch national flag algorithm that efficiently handles duplicate keys
- **Depth monitoring**: Switches to heapsort when recursion depth exceeds 2⌊log₂(n)⌋ to maintain O(n log n) worst-case
- **Space complexity**: O(log n) stack space through tail-call optimization

## Code Example
```javascript
function introsort(arr) {
  const n = arr.length;
  const maxDepth = Math.floor(2 * Math.log2(n || 1));

  function sort(left, right, depth) {
    if (left >= right) return;

    if (depth === 0) {
      heapSort(arr, left, right);
      return;
    }

    const [lt, gt] = threeWayPartition(arr, left, right);

    // Sort smaller partition first (tail recursion optimization)
    if (lt - left < right - gt) {
      sort(left, lt - 1, depth - 1);
      sort(gt + 1, right, depth - 1);
    } else {
      sort(gt + 1, right, depth - 1);
      sort(left, lt - 1, depth - 1);
    }
  }

  sort(0, n - 1, maxDepth);
  return arr;
}
```

